      { "text": "Give a full normothermic dose of intravenous amiodarone to suppress the underlying atrial fibrillation.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Standard antiarrhythmic doses are dangerous in cold patients because drug metabolism is markedly slowed; on rewarming, the accumulated drug can produce overdose toxicity. This would be the correct answer if the patient were normothermic with new symptomatic atrial fibrillation requiring rhythm control." },
      { "text": "Continue gentle rewarming and handling, recognizing that slow atrial fibrillation with Osborn (J) waves is expected in this stage.", "explanation": "Correct. Slow atrial fibrillation is the 'classic' rhythm in hypothermia, the notched J-point wave is the Osborn (J) wave, and a prolonged QT and shivering artifact complete the picture; bradycardia is appropriate because the cold heart has a low metabolic demand. The cold myocardium is highly irritable, so handling should be gentle and rewarming should proceed without provocative interventions, since tachycardia in this setting would actually suggest concurrent bleeding, sepsis, or intoxication." },
      { "text": "Initiate immediate chest compressions because Osborn waves indicate impending cardiac arrest.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Osborn waves do not indicate arrest, and CPR in a hypothermic patient who is still perfusing can itself precipitate ventricular fibrillation in an irritable cold myocardium. This would be the correct answer only when the patient is in confirmed cardiac arrest with no detectable forward flow." },
      { "text": "Cardiovert urgently because slow atrial fibrillation in cold patients carries a high risk of stroke.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Urgent cardioversion is not indicated; the rhythm is the expected hypothermic pattern and the heart is too irritable to manipulate aggressively. This would be the correct answer if the patient were normothermic with new-onset atrial fibrillation and hemodynamic instability from the rhythm itself." }
